course: dictionary
lesson: 13515
title: Impassable and the clues around it
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Impassable is made of two parts. The prefix im- means "not", and pass means to go past or through. So impassable means you cannot get past or through.
explain: «The road was impassable after the heavy snow.» The clues are heavy snow, so cars cannot drive along the road. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The road was impossible to travel along after the heavy snow." It still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: «The river had flooded the bridge, so the path was impassable.» Flooded tells you the water blocks the way. Impassable often means blocked, flooded or too rough to cross.
fact: Impassable means impossible to go through or travel along.
fact: A road, path or pass can be impassable because it is blocked, flooded or too rough.
fact: «The road is impassable» means nobody can travel along it right now.
fact: The prefix im- means "not", so impassable is the opposite of passable.
exercise: «The road was impassable after the flood.» What does impassable mean? | Lit up with lamps at night | *Impossible to travel along | Very busy with cars and buses | Freshly painted and bright
exercise: News report: «The bridge is impassable.» Which reason fits best? | It is full of people waiting to cross | It is very short and narrow | It has been painted bright red | *It is blocked by fallen trees
exercise: «The road is impassable.» Which word could replace impassable and keep the meaning? | friendly | quiet | *blocked | bright
test:
«After the storm, the mountain pass was impassable, so the lorries turned back.» What does impassable tell us about the pass? | Lorries are being painted there | *Lorries cannot get through it | The pass is brand new and smooth | The lorries were late for lunch
Which of these is most likely to be impassable? | A quiet park with birds singing | *A road under metres of flood water | A bench in a sunny playground | A bright, empty classroom
exam:
Mia says, «The jungle path was impassable, so we used the river instead.» What does impassable tell you about the path? | It was the perfect place for a picnic | It was too bright to see | *It was too blocked or rough to travel along | It was the newest path in the park
